{"author":{"id":"a51b5961cc6de343a64125d8eb6a0f965aa434020b9c46ed932a1bbf143eb532"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791709716,"nonce":"WczCLFZOoXxq8HVHFzCwvBZ99/Q=","title":"The wiki holds 300-500 MB: what is retained should follow what is drawn","message":"The user saw about 500 MB for the wiki and expects it under 150 MB, given that what is retained follows what is drawn. Measured with the process's physical footprint (what Activity Monitor shows: `footprint \u003cpid\u003e`, or `memoryFootprint()` now), real window, Retina 1120x780 at scale 2, macOS 26.6.\n\nBefore (JS leak already fixed in 6e7e9b0), overview -\u003e every page -\u003e overview, three rounds: footprint 342 MB, of which:\n- graphics: 152 MB (IOAccelerator plus owned unmapped graphics)\n- malloc: 86 MB (MALLOC_SMALL plus MALLOC_LARGE)\n- V8: 43 MB\n- IOSurface (swapchain drawables): 41 MB\n\n- **Renderer scratch textures.** Every layer is a full-window RGBA texture (2240x1560x4 = 14 MB at 2x). The renderer kept as many as the deepest frame ever needed, plus `rows` (blur/backdrop) and `shadow`, until the window resized.\n- **Image atlas.** It grows to 4096² (64 MB) on the Glass and Images pages at 2x, never shrinks, and is emptied only when full.\n- **V8 young generation.** new_space sat at 64 MB physical with nothing live: the semi-space had grown to its maximum from allocation churn. `--max-semi-space-size=4` takes about 110 MB off the plateau. `v8.setFlagsFromString` at run time has no effect; it must be on the command line.\n- **Imports.** typegpu (19 MB) and the Vue runtime (15 MB) were imported at start by the canvas-shader and vue-counter examples.\n- **Transient big buffers.** A frame that resamples many images allocated a new Uint8Array per atlas entry. Freed large blocks stay in the macOS allocator's footprint: in plain node, 20 x 4.5 MB ArrayBuffers freed and GC'd leave footprint at 101 MB, and `malloc_zone_pressure_relief` does not return them. They are reused, not leaked, but every burst sets a high-water mark.\n- **Glyph atlas.** 1024², about 1 MB. Not a factor. (blinc_abi never calls gc_atlas; irrelevant at this size.)\n- **Font files.** Memory-mapped by fontdb, so clean pages, not footprint.\n\n- **Glass backdrop:** one half-resolution texture for all glass, captured once a frame, with blur in the glass fragment shader.\n- **Effect layers:** rendered into tight-bounds textures from a LayerTextureCache in size buckets. After 60 idle frames it drops one texture per bucket per frame; after 120 frames it drops them all.\n- **Atlases:** the SVG atlas is mark-and-sweep with a repack at 75% full; the glyph atlas runs an epoch GC at 85% utilisation.\n- **Budget:** the memory budget exists but `enforce_memory_budget` has no callers.\n\nWe do not take the half-resolution backdrop: the user requires no loss of quality, and the clear-glass demos depend on sharp detail behind the glass.\n\n- **Renderer.** Each scratch texture (layer by depth, rows, shadow) records the last frame that needed it. One unused for 60 frames is released. `trim()` releases what the last frame did not use. When the images the last frame drew fill under a quarter of the atlas, or less than its size needs, trim empties the atlas and remakes it at that size; the next frame refills it at full resolution. A single scratch buffer serves every atlas entry.\n- **Window.** It trims its renderer once the scene has been quiet for a second, and asks the allocator to hand back what is free (`releaseFreeMemory`: macOS pressure relief, glibc malloc_trim, Windows HeapCompact).\n- **Stats.** `memoryFootprint()` (macOS phys_footprint, Linux statm resident, Windows PrivateUsage), `SceneRenderStats.textureBytes` and `NativeWindowHost.frameTime`.\n- **Wiki.** It loads the Vue and TypeGPU examples on first show, and its scripts run node with `--max-semi-space-size=4`. A performance panel in the header shows footprint, this page's growth and peak, JS heap, GPU textures, nodes, frame time and frames per second.\n\n| | Graphics | Footprint |\n|---|---|---|\n| Overview at start | | 103-108 MB (was 114) |\n| After every page, back on the overview, trimmed | ~28 MB (was 152) | 214-250 MB (was 342) |\n| After visiting only the Paint page, back on the overview | | 149 MB |\n\nEach page alone, while it shows, in a fresh process:\n- most pages: 130-165 MB\n- Glass: 292-396 MB, with 121 MB of renderer textures\n- Images: 349-411 MB, with 121 MB of renderer textures\n- Paint: 295-350 MB, with 55 MB of renderer textures\n\n- **Layers sized to their bounds, as Blinc does.** This is the large one for Paint and Glass. It needs an offset in the viewport uniform per layer, one uniform slot and bind group per depth, and the composite, rows and shadow passes to map the layer rect.\n- **The atlas at 2x on image-heavy pages.** Images are resampled on the CPU to their on-screen size at the device scale and kept in the atlas, and the full decode is held too (planet.webp alone is 12.6 MB decoded). Options:\n  - Drop the CPU decode once its atlas entries exist, and decode again on demand.\n  - Size the wiki's assets to their largest drawn size at 2x.\n  - Per-image GPU textures with mipmaps. Quality must be compared against the CPU resample before choosing this.\n- **Allocator high-water from heavy pages.** It stays after leaving them; reducing peak live use per page is the lever.\n- **Transitions of transform** blend as matrices (see the keyframe rotate issue).\n- **Linux and Windows numbers.** The NUC can measure headless (lavapipe); Windows cannot be tested yet.","files":null}]}